        <h2 id="trains">Trains from Walton-on-Thames</h2>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames has frequent South Western Railway services towards London and the wider south-west network. The exact timetable changes between weekday peaks, off-peak periods, evenings, Sundays and periods of engineering work.</p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/station/walton-on-thames-station-platform-2.webp" alt="A red South Western Railway train standing at Platform 2 at Walton-on-Thames with its doors open at sunset, as passengers walk away along the platform towards the footbridge" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="1050" />
          <figcaption>A South Western Railway service at Platform 2, with the low sun behind the footbridge. Photograph by Darren Bayley, 17 March 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>To London Waterloo, Platform 1</h3>
        <p>Direct services run throughout the day. The fastest trains usually take about 22 to 25 minutes; services making more stops commonly take around 30 to 35 minutes, depending on the service, calling at some or all of Surbiton, Wimbledon, Clapham Junction and Vauxhall before Waterloo. Clapham Junction connects onward to Victoria, Gatwick Airport and south London; Vauxhall connects with the Victoria line; Wimbledon connects with the District line and London Trams.</p>

        <h3>Towards Weybridge, Woking and beyond, Platform 2</h3>
        <p>Direct destinations can include Weybridge, Woking, Guildford, Basingstoke and Portsmouth Harbour, with Southampton and other south-west destinations sometimes requiring a change. Woking is the main interchange for onward journeys towards Guildford, Portsmouth, Southampton, Bournemouth, Salisbury and Exeter.</p>

        <p>For an exact journey, use the <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/plan-my-journey" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Western Railway journey planner ↗</a> or the <a href="https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Rail journey planner ↗</a>. This page deliberately doesn't hard-code first and last train times, since they change too often and can be affected by engineering work.</p>

        <h2>Live departures and disruption</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/live-trains/departures/walton-on-thames/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Live departures from Walton-on-Thames ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/plan-my-journey" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Western Railway service updates ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/status-and-disruptions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Rail planned engineering work ↗</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>During planned closures, rail replacement buses normally leave from the station forecourt off Station Avenue.</p>

        <h2 id="tickets">Tickets, Oyster, contactless and smartcards</h2>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames has a staffed ticket office and ticket machines, and accepts tickets, eTickets and compatible smartcards.</p>

        <h3>Oyster and contactless</h3>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames is outside the London fare zones, and neither Oyster cards nor ordinary contactless pay-as-you-go tap-in/tap-out travel are accepted. A contactless bank card can still be used to pay for a ticket at a machine or the ticket office, which is a different thing from tapping directly at the gate.</p>

        <h3>SWR Touch Smartcard and Tap2Go</h3>
        <p>The SWR Touch Smartcard is a free, reusable card available from the ticket office while you wait, or ordered through an SWR online account for delivery normally within five working days. Tickets bought online or via the SWR app can be loaded onto it. The same card also supports Tap2Go, SWR's own pay-as-you-go product; it isn't Oyster, and on its own it doesn't extend onto the London Underground, London Overground or London buses.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/train-tickets/smart-ticketing/touch-smartcard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get an SWR Touch Smartcard ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/train-tickets/tap2go" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tap2Go eligibility and terms ↗</a></li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Ticket office hours</h3>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Hours</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Monday to Saturday</td><td>06:10 to 20:40</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sunday</td><td>08:10 to 19:40</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Ticket machines are available whenever the office is closed. Hours may differ on public holidays; check <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/travelling-with-us/at-the-station/walton-on-thames" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SWR's Walton-on-Thames station page ↗</a> for the current times.</p>

        <h2>Station entrances and layout</h2>
        <p>The station sits between Station Avenue on the north side and Mayfield Road on the south side, close to the boundary between Walton-on-Thames and Hersham.</p>
        <p>The main <strong>Station Avenue entrance</strong>, beside Platform 1, has the ticket office, main forecourt, taxi rank and principal cycle storage, and is the most convenient entrance from Walton town centre and Ashley Park. Platform 1 is reached from here by a ramp of around 1:12; lifts and the footbridge give an accessible route to Platform 2 when they're operating.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Mayfield Road entrance</strong> gives direct access to Platform 2 from the Hersham and Burwood Park side, via a longer ramp of around 1:20, and serves as an alternative step-free route to Platform 2 if the lift or footbridge is out of action. The road route between the two entrances is longer than crossing via the footbridge.</p>
        <p>A modern footbridge connects the platforms, with lifts and new stairs added as part of a £6 million Access for All project completed in August 2024 (see <a href="#history">History</a>). The older subway is not the station's accessible route.</p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/station/walton-on-thames-station-underpass.webp" alt="The older pedestrian subway at Walton-on-Thames station: a long, low, white-painted tunnel with a curved ceiling and strip lighting, ending in a flight of steps" loading="lazy" width="1000" height="1333" />
          <figcaption>The older subway under the tracks, which ends in a flight of steps and is not the station's step-free route. Photograph by Darren Bayley, 24 August 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames is officially classified as a <strong>Category B1 step-free station</strong>: both operational platforms have step-free access, though the route depends on which entrance you use and whether the lifts are working.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Lifts between the platforms and footbridge</li>
          <li>Ramped access to Platform 1 from Station Avenue, and a longer ramp to Platform 2 from Mayfield Road</li>
          <li>Accessible ticket machines and an induction loop</li>
          <li>Passenger help points on both platforms, with visual departure screens and audio announcements</li>
          <li>A boarding ramp and a wheelchair available for passenger use</li>
          <li>An accessible drop-off point</li>
          <li>An accessible toilet in the Platform 1 booking hall (RADAR/National Key, normally available only while the ticket office is open)</li>
        </ul>
        <figure>
          <img src="/images/station/walton-on-thames-station-footbridge-from-platform-2.webp" alt="The arched steel footbridge at Walton-on-Thames station spanning the four tracks, with a lift tower at each end and a platform running below it" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="788" />
          <figcaption>The Access for All footbridge and its two lift towers, seen from Platform 2 in March 2024, five months before the scheme was formally opened. Photograph by Darren Bayley, 23 March 2024.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>South Western Railway's own station page lists a Changing Places toilet as <strong>not</strong> available at Walton-on-Thames.</p>
        <p>If a lift is out of service, Platform 1 remains reachable from Station Avenue and Platform 2 from Mayfield Road; reaching the opposite platform then means a road journey around the railway rather than crossing the footbridge.</p>

        <h3>Passenger assistance</h3>
        <p>South Western Railway provides boarding and alighting assistance while services are running, normally arranged through Passenger Assist. The published SWR assistance number is <strong>0800 528 2100</strong>, and assistance can usually be booked up to two hours before travel. See <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/travelling-with-us/assisted-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Passenger Assist information ↗</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="parking">Parking</h2>
        <p>There's a paid station car park, generally open 24 hours. Published sources currently disagree on its size: National Rail and South Western Railway list <strong>140 spaces</strong>, including two accessible bays, while the car park operator, APCOA, lists <strong>250 spaces</strong>. We haven't been able to resolve that discrepancy this session, so treat the operator's own page as the most current word on availability.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Parking period</th><th>Published price</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Peak day (Monday to Friday)</td><td>£6.60</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Off-peak, from 12:00</td><td>£3.30</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Saturday, Sunday or bank holiday</td><td>£3.30</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Hourly off-peak, via RingGo</td><td>£1.10</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Weekly</td><td>£26.30</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Monthly</td><td>£92.00</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Quarterly</td><td>£276.00</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Annual</td><td>£920.00</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Complimentary parking is available to registered Blue Badge holders under the operator's scheme. For current prices, payment methods and the RingGo location code, see the <a href="https://www.apcoa.co.uk/find-parking/locations/walton-upon-thames/walton-upon-thames-station-walton-upon-thames" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">APCOA Walton-on-Thames station car park page ↗</a>.</p>

        <h3>Alternative public car parks</h3>
        <p>Elmbridge Borough Council separately operates car parks nearby on Station Avenue and Mayfield Road, with their own restrictions and charges.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.elmbridge.gov.uk/parking-and-roads/car-parks/find-car-park/station-avenue-car-park" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Station Avenue car park ↗</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.elmbridge.gov.uk/parking-and-roads/car-parks/find-car-park/mayfield-road-car-park" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mayfield Road car park ↗</a></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Drop-off, pick-up and taxis</h2>
        <p>The main forecourt off Station Avenue handles drop-off, pick-up, taxis and rail replacement services, and has a taxi rank. The Mayfield Road entrance, on the opposite side of the railway, isn't directly connected to the forecourt by road.</p>

        <h2 id="buses">Buses</h2>
        <p>Bus stops outside the station link to Walton town centre, Hersham, Esher, Kingston, Shepperton, Staines and nearby communities. National Rail's current onward travel poster lists routes <strong>458</strong> and <strong>459</strong> serving destinations including Hersham, Esher, Kingston, Thames Ditton, Lower Halliford, Shepperton, Laleham, Staines and Whiteley Village; the poster's own stop-letter assignments are the most reliable source for exactly which stop to use for each direction, so we've linked it below rather than reproduce the detail here. Older station guides sometimes list routes 461, 555 or 564, but these don't appear on the current poster.</p>
        <p><a href="https://assets.nationalrail.co.uk/e8xgegruud3g/6W6jfwXTTU5AxDUpGgfw6d/fe8b5a4f2fc7970a154f95a137b5aca3/Walton_on_Thames__WAL_.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View the current National Rail onward travel poster for Walton-on-Thames ↗</a></p>

        <h2>Walking to Walton town centre</h2>
        <p>The station isn't in Walton town centre; allow approximately 20 to 25 minutes to walk to the central shopping area, mainly along Ashley Park Road and Ashley Road through a residential area. The River Thames, Walton Bridge and the riverside lie beyond the town centre. The station also serves Ashley Park, the Halfway area, north Hersham and parts of Burwood Park; Hersham itself has a separate station on the branch towards Hampton Court.</p>

        <h2>Cycling</h2>
        <p>There are 132 sheltered, CCTV-covered cycle storage spaces outside the main entrance, accessible 24 hours a day with basic maintenance tools available. South Western Railway doesn't list a station cycle-hire scheme, and restricts full-size bicycles on some peak services; see the <a href="https://www.southwesternrailway.com/travelling-with-us/travelling-with-a-bike" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SWR bicycle policy ↗</a> for current rules.</p>

        <h2 id="facilities">Facilities</h2>
        <h3>Ticketing and information</h3>
        <p>Staffed ticket office, self-service machines, ticket gates, live departure screens, public-address announcements, help points on both platforms, CCTV, station Wi-Fi and an induction loop.</p>
        <h3>Waiting and refreshments</h3>
        <p>A heated waiting area in the booking hall, and a coffee shop on Platform 1. There are no other retail units at the station.</p>
        <h3>Toilets</h3>
        <p>Standard toilets and baby-changing facilities, plus a RADAR/National-Key accessible toilet in the Platform 1 booking hall, normally available only during ticket office hours. There is no Changing Places toilet.</p>
        <h3>Luggage and lost property</h3>
        <p>There's no left-luggage facility or luggage-trolley service. Lost property is handled through South Western Railway's central process associated with London Waterloo, rather than a dedicated office at Walton-on-Thames.</p>

        <h2 id="history">History</h2>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames is one of the oldest railway stations in Surrey, opening on <strong>21 May 1838</strong> as part of the London and Southampton Railway's first public section, from Nine Elms in London to Woking Common. The line's London terminus moved to Waterloo in 1848, creating the direct approach into central London that the station still uses today.</p>

        <p>According to research prepared for this page, the station's name has since changed several times, reflecting its position between Walton and Hersham; we haven't been able to independently verify the exact sequence of dates against a primary source this session, so the table below should be read as reported rather than independently confirmed.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Reported name</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>From 1838</td><td>Walton</td></tr>
              <tr><td>From 1849</td><td>Walton &amp; Hersham</td></tr>
              <tr><td>From 1913</td><td>Walton for Hersham</td></tr>
              <tr><td>From 1935</td><td>Walton-on-Thames</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>As traffic grew, the line through Walton developed into today's four-track railway; Platforms 1 and 2 use the outer lines, while the two former central platforms, beside the fast lines, are out of use, gated and inaccessible, with non-stopping trains passing through the centre of the station at up to 90mph. Full electric services on the extensions towards Woking, Guildford and Portsmouth began in July 1937, part of the wider Southern Railway electrification of the route in the 1930s. The railway helped establish Walton as a commuter town, with residential development spreading around Ashley Park, the Halfway area and the station approaches as regular travel to London became practical.</p>

