          <img src="/images/station/walton-on-thames-station-platform-1-viewed-from-platform-2.webp" alt="Platform 1 at Walton-on-Thames seen from Platform 2 across the tracks, its long canopied building set behind the brick face of a disused centre platform carrying a red Do not alight here sign" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="1050" />
          <figcaption>Platform 1 seen from Platform 2, with the gated, out-of-use centre platform in front of it. Photograph by Darren Bayley, 25 August 2026.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>The 2024 Access for All project</h3>
        <p>A £6 million Access for All scheme began in September 2022 and was formally opened on 30 August 2024, delivering a new footbridge, two lifts, new staircases, a widened Platform 1 and upgraded CCTV, giving passengers a step-free route between the platforms for the first time. See <a href="https://www.networkrailmediacentre.co.uk/news/where-theres-a-walton-theres-a-way-new-lifts-and-bridge-make-walton-on-thames-station-accessible" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Network Rail's project announcement ↗</a>.</p>

        <figure>
          <img src="/images/station/walton-on-thames-station-platform-2-footbridge.webp" alt="The new arched steel footbridge at Walton-on-Thames station, with a lift shaft and a new staircase with yellow handrails still enclosed by contractors' fencing" loading="lazy" width="1400" height="1050" />
          <figcaption>The new footbridge and staircase during the Access for All works, with contractors' fencing still in place. Photograph by Darren Bayley, 23 December 2023.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>The March 2024 derailment</h3>
        <p>At around 05:40 on 4 March 2024, the 04:54 South Western Railway service from Basingstoke to London Waterloo, travelling at around 85mph, struck a section of redundant rail that had been left too close to the running line on the approach to Walton-on-Thames after engineering work. The leading wheelset derailed, though the train remained upright and came to a stand around 500 metres beyond the point of impact; around 100 passengers were on board, and no injuries were reported. The Rail Accident Investigation Branch found that checks carried out before the railway was handed back for normal running hadn't identified the hazard, and published its final report, 04/2025, in February 2025. See the <a href="https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/67b309ce421271d7e45f57e6/R042025_250217_Walton-on-Thames.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RAIB report ↗</a>.</p>

        <h2>How busy is the station?</h2>
        <p>The Office of Rail and Road estimated <strong>2,425,886 passenger entries and exits in 2024/25</strong>, a figure that measures journeys entering and leaving the station rather than unique people.</p>
